\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{CIVO mechanism.}
CIVO propagates through multiple pathways: Walker Walk develops a $1.63\times$ reward prediction gap while Cheetah Run shows none ($1.00\times$) despite higher severity ($8.27\times$ NVE). The mechanism is analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced; unlike MPC, imagination-based actor-critic bootstraps value targets entirely within the learned model without real-data correction (Appendix~\ref{sec:mpc_immune}). This is not generic compounding error \citep{janner2019mbpo}: untrained embeddings at $\dt{=}2$ produce no overestimation (NVE${<}0.6\times$), confirming \emph{systematic} bias from trained embeddings.

\paragraph{Role of embedding training (ablation evidence).}
Frozen embeddings participate identically in the forward pass yet do not trigger CIVO (0/16 controls vs.\ 5/7 trained, $p < 0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:causal_ablation}): the vulnerability lies in what the embedding \emph{learns}, not in conditioning architecture.

\paragraph{Why standard countermeasures fail.}
CIVO is an emergent property of trained temporal embeddings co-adapting with the world model, not a single-pathway defect. SGS (gradient): pathway redundancy shifts co-adaptation to unblocked channels. RAVE (output): the EMA anchor tracks biased values during onset. TCN (representation): binary on/off behavior suggests a phase transition. The only reliable \emph{elimination}---freezing the embedding (0/16 controls)---degrades performance to 22--33\% of baseline, destroying temporal conditioning entirely.

\paragraph{Why architectural intervention partially succeeds.}
The $2{\times}2$ critic factorial isolates two necessary components: min-aggregation enables recovery but not prevention; capacity alone provides neither; their combination achieves prevention in 3/4 seeds (NVE $\leq 1.4\times$). Incomplete Cheetah transfer (0/1, CIVO at $70\times$) suggests a severity ceiling. Variable-rate imagination remains open; monitor via NVE ($\geq 2.0\times$; Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}). CIVO is specific to multi-joint locomotion (5/7 seeds; Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_env_appendix}).

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
\label{sec:detector}
Three guidelines follow. (1)~Monitor NVE throughout training: a sustained rise above $2.0\times$ indicates CIVO onset and should trigger rollback; a lightweight online variant monitoring superlinear NVE growth achieves TPR~$5/5$ but FPR~$2/9$ ($22\%$; Algorithm~\ref{alg:civo_detector}, Appendix~\ref{sec:detector_appendix}). (2)~Use frozen-embedding runs as a diagnostic control---if frozen matches trained, the embedding fuels co-adaptation rather than temporal reasoning. (3)~Wide dual-critic (min $+$ capacity) is the only partial prevention (3/4); scaling this approach or decoupling the embedding from value prediction are promising directions.

